 "overview": "This design changes the existing layered FastAPI/SQLAlchemy URL shortener. It does not redesign it. CR-142 adds (a) client-chosen aliases (3-32 chars [A-Za-z0-9_-], case preserved; reserved words give 422, a taken code gives 409) and (b) an optional future `expires_at` (redirecting an expired link gives 410 'Link has expired'; metadata gains `expires_at` and `is_expired`). Everything is additive. Existing endpoints, fields, status codes, headers, router order and the 165 existing tests are preserved.\n\nSchema change (explicit): `links.expires_at DATETIME NULL` (generic sqlalchemy DateTime, naive UTC). Startup runs Base.metadata.create_all and then a new idempotent `ensure_schema(engine)` in app/core/db.py. If the column is missing, it runs the literal `ALTER TABLE links ADD COLUMN expires_at DATETIME NULL`. PostgreSQL has no DATETIME type, so on postgresql it runs the literal `ALTER TABLE links ADD COLUMN expires_at TIMESTAMP NULL` instead (see ADR-4). Existing rows get NULL and never expire. No change is needed to links.code (already VARCHAR(32), UNIQUE uq_links_code) or to idempotency_keys DDL. What request_hash covers widens, but stays backwards compatible.\n\nLayering: format and type validation happen in app/schemas (alias pattern; ISO-8601-only expires_at normalised to aware UTC). Business rules happen in app/services/link_service.py: the reserved-word check against codes.RESERVED_CODES (single source of truth, which now includes 'metrics'), the expires_at-in-the-future check against an injectable clock, the alias single-insert path (IntegrityError gives 409), the backwards-compatible idempotency hash, and the expiry check on redirect (from the loaded row, no extra query). Lookups on all code-addressed endpoints use the new codes.is_valid_lookup_code (^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$). codes.is_valid_code_format stays base62-only. HEAD /{code} becomes visible in OpenAPI so its 410 is documented (FR-13).\n\nBUG-77: no code change to validate_target_url (inv